Q1. What is Unit Testing?

Interview Ready Answer

Unit Testing is a software testing technique in which the smallest
testable unit of an application, such as a method, function, or small
class, is tested independently to verify that it works correctly.

It is usually performed by developers before integration testing to
detect bugs early.

Key Points

• Tests the smallest unit of code.
• Performed independently.
• Usually done by developers.
• Detects bugs early.
• Improves code quality.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Unit Testing?

Interview Ready Answer

Unit Testing is required to verify that each individual unit of code
works correctly before integrating it with other modules.

It helps detect defects early, making debugging, maintenance, and
future code changes much easier.

Advantages

• Detects bugs at an early stage.
• Improves software quality.
• Easier debugging.
• Easier maintenance.
• Safer refactoring.
• Gives developers confidence while modifying code.

Real-Life Example

Banking Application

Transfer Money

↓

Validate Account

↓

Check Balance

↓

Deduct Amount

↓

Send Notification

If the Check Balance() method has a bug, Unit Testing detects it
before the complete application is integrated.

======================================================================

Q3. Difference between Unit Testing and Integration Testing.

Interview Ready Answer

Unit Testing

• Tests a single method, function, or class.
• Checks individual business logic.
• Performed before Integration Testing.
• Fast execution.
• Easier to debug.

Example

Testing calculateSalary() method.

------------------------------------------------------------

Integration Testing

• Tests multiple modules together.
• Checks communication between modules.
• Performed after Unit Testing.
• Slower than Unit Testing.
• Harder to debug.

Example

Testing Login Module → Service → Database.

======================================================================

Q4. What is a Unit in Unit Testing?

Interview Ready Answer

A Unit is the smallest testable part of an application.

It is usually:

• A method
• A function
• A small class

Each unit is tested independently without depending on other modules.

